{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "03-natural-deduction.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Proofs as Lean tactics",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Prove propositional statements in Lean by asking, for each connective, what it takes to prove one and what you can do with one.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A truth table checks a formula by trying every row. That stops working for \"every natural number $n$ satisfies $n + 0 = n$\": the rows never end. A **proof** derives a statement by rules instead, each a small step that is obviously valid, and Lean checks every step. What should the rules be?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "What each connective needs",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> To convince someone of $p \\land q$, what must you hand them? If someone hands you a proof of $p \\land q$, what can you get out of it? Ask the same of $p \\lor q$ and of $p \\to q$ before reading on.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "- $p \\land q$: hand over a proof of each, a pair. Given one, you can take either half.\n- $p \\lor q$: hand over a proof of one of them, saying which. Given one, you do not know which side you have, so you must finish the job in both cases.\n- $p \\to q$: hand over a method that turns any proof of $p$ into a proof of $q$; to build it, assume $p$ and derive $q$. Given one and a proof of $p$, apply it and get $q$.\n- $\\lnot p$ is $p \\to \\bot$: a method that turns any proof of $p$ into a contradiction.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Introduction and elimination\n> Natural deduction is these answers written as rules: for each connective, how to **introduce** it (prove it) and how to **eliminate** it (use it). In Lean, `intro` assumes the premise of $\\to$, and applying `h hp` uses one; `constructor` or `⟨hp, hq⟩` builds $\\land$, and `h.1`, `h.2` take it apart; `left` and `right` build $\\lor$, and `cases` uses it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "example (p q : Prop) (hp : p) (hq : q) : p ∧ q := by\n  constructor\n  · exact hp\n  · exact hq\n\nexample (p q : Prop) : p ∧ q → q := by\n  intro h\n  exact h.2\n\nexample (p q : Prop) : p → p ∨ q := by\n  intro hp\n  left\n  exact hp",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Put the cursor after a tactic to see the goal it leaves: the hypotheses above the line, what is left to prove below it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Replace `sorry` with a proof. Lean checks it as you type; the exercise is done when Lean accepts it with nothing left unproved.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem and_swap' (p q : Prop) : p ∧ q → q ∧ p := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove that $\\land$ commutes.",
      "leanSolution": "  intro ⟨hp, hq⟩\n  exact ⟨hq, hp⟩",
      "hints": [
        "`intro ⟨hp, hq⟩` takes the conjunction apart as it introduces it.",
        "`exact ⟨hq, hp⟩` builds the conjunction the other way round."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem or_swap' (p q : Prop) : p ∨ q → q ∨ p := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove that $\\lor$ commutes.",
      "leanSolution": "  intro h\n  cases h with\n  | inl hp => exact Or.inr hp\n  | inr hq => exact Or.inl hq",
      "hints": [
        "After `intro h`, `cases h` gives two goals: one with `hp : p`, one with `hq : q`.",
        "`Or.inr hp` proves `q ∨ p` from `p`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem modus_tollens (p q : Prop) : (p → q) → ¬q → ¬p := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove *modus tollens*: if $p$ implies $q$ and $q$ is false, then $p$ is false.",
      "leanSolution": "  intro hpq hnq hp\n  exact hnq (hpq hp)",
      "hints": [
        "`¬p` is `p → False`, so `intro` can take a proof of `p` too.",
        "`hpq hp` is a proof of `q`, and `hnq` turns it into `False`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem curry' (p q r : Prop) : (p ∧ q → r) → (p → q → r) := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove that a function of a pair can take its arguments one at a time.",
      "leanSolution": "  intro h hp hq\n  exact h ⟨hp, hq⟩",
      "hints": [
        "Introduce all three hypotheses, then feed `h` the pair."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem not_or_split (p q : Prop) : ¬(p ∨ q) → ¬p ∧ ¬q := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove one of De Morgan's laws: if neither $p \\lor q$, then not $p$ and not $q$.",
      "leanSolution": "  intro h\n  exact ⟨fun hp => h (Or.inl hp), fun hq => h (Or.inr hq)⟩",
      "hints": [
        "A proof of `¬p` is a function from `p` to `False`.",
        "Given `hp : p`, `Or.inl hp` proves `p ∨ q`, which `h` refutes."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Proofs are programs",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!note] Curry–Howard\n> \"A method that turns any proof of $p$ into a proof of $q$\" is a function, and in Lean it is one: the term `fun hp => h (Or.inl hp)` from the last exercise takes a proof of $p$ to a proof of $\\bot$. A proof of $p \\land q$ is a pair. Propositions are types and proofs are their values; checking a proof is type checking. The λ-calculus courses come back to this.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "example (p q : Prop) : p ∧ q → q ∧ p := fun ⟨hp, hq⟩ => ⟨hq, hp⟩",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!mistake]\n> It is natural to expect every tautology to have a proof by these rules. $\\lnot\\lnot p \\to p$ is a tautology:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "taut(¬¬p → p)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Yet the rules above give no proof of it. A proof of $\\lnot\\lnot p$ is a function that turns refutations of $p$ into contradictions, and nothing in it is evidence for $p$. It needs one more principle, the excluded middle (`Classical.em p : p ∨ ¬p`), or proof by contradiction:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem dne (p : Prop) : ¬¬p → p := by\n  intro h\n  exact Classical.byContradiction h",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Each connective's rules say what it takes to prove it and what you can do with it, and Lean's tactics are those rules. A proof of an implication is a function; a proof of a conjunction is a pair. Excluded middle is the one extra principle classical logic adds.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "So far every statement was about $p$ and $q$. Most statements in mathematics are about every number, or some number. The next lesson adds $\\forall$ and $\\exists$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
